WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:30.730
<v Ira Glass>I had the strangest experience last night. I have these tapes, recorded on my answering machine 10 years ago. Tapes that I had not heard in a decade until last night. And I don't think I can play these tapes for you. I don't think that the people on them would give the permission. On one tape, there's a brief conversation between me and this woman who I lived with for seven years, accidentally recorded by the answering machine just as we were splitting up.
<v Ira Glass>我昨晚经历了最奇怪的事情。我有一些十年前录在答录机里的磁带。直到昨晚，我都有十年没听过这些磁带了。而且我不认为我可以把这些磁带放给你听。我不认为录音里的人会同意。其中一盘磁带里，有我跟我同居了七年的那个女人之间的一段简短对话，是在我们分手时被答录机无意中录下来的。

00:00:30.730 --> 00:01:20.490
<v Ira Glass>And on this tape, she's calling me from the street to say that she's going to a movie with a friend. And I tell her I'd like to come along. She's Gotta Have It is the movie that they're seeing. And she indicates no, that I'm not welcome. And then I tell her, "Well, I guess maybe I really do have too much work to do and some laundry that I have to get done." And later in the conversation, she asks if I'm going to be up late, and I say no. And she says, "Well, I thought you said you had all this work to do," like she's catching me in a lie. I mean, she is. She is catching me in a lie. But you know, a lie that I kind of made at her bidding. And then she asked me if I'm absolutely sure that I'm going to get her laundry done that night. And I tell her I'm sure.
<v Ira Glass>在这盘磁带里，她从街上打电话给我，说她要和朋友去看电影。她们要看的是《她一定要有它》(She's Gotta Have It)。她表示不希望我去。然后我对她说：“好吧，我想我确实还有太多工作要做，还有些衣服得洗。”后来在谈话中，她问我是不是要熬夜，我说不。她说：“嗯，我还以为你说你有很多工作要做呢。”听起来她好像在拆穿我的谎言。我的意思是，她确实是在拆穿我。但你知道，那谎言是我应她的要求而编的。然后她问我是不是绝对确定那天晚上会帮她把衣服洗好。我告诉她我确定。

00:01:20.490 --> 00:01:50.520
<v Ira Glass>It was hard to listen to. I can hear myself being so scared of her in this tape. And in this same conversation, she talks about this guy who, just a few weeks later, she got involved with, her next serious relationship after me. And I talk about this woman who I was just about to start seeing in this serious way, after her. And that part of the conversation is very awkward, very awkward.
<v Ira Glass>听这些录音很痛苦。我能听到自己在磁带里是多么害怕她。而在同一段对话中，她谈到了那个男的，仅仅几周后，她就和他好上了，那是我之后她的下一段认真关系。我也谈到了那个我刚要开始认真交往的女人。那部分对话非常尴尬，非常尴尬。

00:01:50.520 --> 00:02:22.980
<v Ira Glass>And before I heard this tape, I could not have remembered much about that summer, that summer where we were splitting up in 1988 after seven years. But hearing the tape, it all came back. Where we lived, what was in the apartment, what we used to wear, how we talked to each other, and how I felt all the time when we were together, this way that I don't feel anymore. And it messed me up.
<v Ira Glass>在听到这盘磁带之前，关于那个夏天，也就是我们1988年相处七年后分手的那个夏天，我几乎记不清什么了。但听到磁带后，一切都回来了。我们住的地方，公寓里的东西，我们过去常穿的衣服，我们互相交谈的方式，以及我们在一起时我那种始终如一的感受——那是我现在再也没有的感觉。它让我心乱如麻。

00:02:22.980 --> 00:03:14.570
<v Ira Glass>And it wasn't like looking at photos. Pictures are posed. Pictures are these tiny little-- they're tiny. You can hold them in your hand. They're 3-by-5. You can crush a picture. This was not posed. And it was not small. And part of that, I think, is just the power of recordings. And part of it was the fact that we were on the phone. There is something about being on the telephone. It's just so intimate. Talking to a person on the phone, you are right there. You are so close. It's like you're whispering in each other's ears.
<v Ira Glass>这不像是看照片。照片是摆拍的。照片很小，非常小。你可以把它们拿在手里。它们只有3乘5英寸大。你可以把照片揉皱。而这些录音不是摆拍的，也不是微不足道的。我认为这部分是因为录音本身的力量。另一部分原因是我们在打电话。打电话这种事很特别，它非常亲密。在电话里和人交谈，你就仿佛身临其境。你们靠得那么近，就像是在彼此耳边低语。

00:03:14.570 --> 00:03:49.700
<v Ira Glass>There are these tapes of Lyndon Johnson on the telephone in this audio book called Taking Charge by historian Michael Beschloss. And Beschloss makes the argument that without these recordings of Lyndon Johnson on the telephone, an important part of Johnson would be completely lost to history. These tapes are so raw. We hear him operating on people in this way that is so-- Beschloss makes the argument that it rarely comes through in transcripts of meetings or public events, him just trying to charm people and flatter them and play on their weaknesses and strong-arm them.
<v Ira Glass>在历史学家迈克尔·贝施洛斯(Michael Beschloss)的有声读物《掌权》(Taking Charge)中，有林登·约翰逊(Lyndon Johnson)的电话录音。贝施洛斯指出，如果没有这些林登·约翰逊打电话的录音，约翰逊的一个重要侧面将从历史上完全消失。这些磁带非常真实。我们听到他以这种方式操纵别人——贝施洛斯认为，这种方式很少出现在会议纪要或公开活动中——他只是试图去吸引人们、奉承他们、利用他们的弱点并强迫他们。

00:03:49.700 --> 00:04:11.790
<v Ira Glass>And he's more honest on the phone than he was in public. In public, Johnson said that he believed the Warren Commission report. On the telephone, he admitted he didn't believe in it. He didn't believe that a lone assassin, Lee Harvey Oswald, acted alone killing JFK. In public, Johnson supported the Vietnam War. On the telephone, he admitted his doubts.
<v Ira Glass>他在电话里比在公开场合更诚实。公开场合中，约翰逊说他相信沃伦委员会的调查报告。而在电话里，他承认他不相信。他不相信李·哈维·奥斯瓦尔德(Lee Harvey Oswald)是独自刺杀肯尼迪的孤狼刺客。公开场合中，约翰逊支持越战。而在电话里，他承认了自己的怀疑。

00:04:11.790 --> 00:04:28.450
<v Lyndon Johnson>I'll tell you, the more I just stayed awake last night thinking about this thing, the more I think of it, I don't know what in the hell-- It looks like, to me, we're getting into another Korea. And it just worries the hell out of me. I don't see what we can ever hope to get out of there with once we're committed.
<v Lyndon Johnson>我跟你说，昨晚我越是躺着想这件事，就越觉得，我不知道到底……在我看来，我们正陷入另一场朝鲜战争。这让我担心极了。一旦我们卷入其中，我不知道我们还能指望怎么脱身。

00:04:28.450 --> 00:04:32.190
<v Ira Glass>He's talking to National Security Advisor McGeorge Bundy.
<v Ira Glass>他正在和国家安全顾问麦乔治·邦迪(McGeorge Bundy)谈话。

00:04:32.190 --> 00:04:36.790
<v Lyndon Johnson>I don't think it's worth fighting for. And I don't think we can get out. And it's just the biggest damn mess--
<v Lyndon Johnson>我不认为这值得一战。我不认为我们能脱身。这简直是天大的烂摊子——

00:04:36.790 --> 00:04:38.250
<v Mcgeorge Bundy>It is. It's an awful mess.
<v Mcgeorge Bundy>确实。这是一个糟糕的烂摊子。

00:04:38.250 --> 00:04:52.410
<v Lyndon Johnson>I look at this sergeant of mine this morning. He's got six little old kids over there. And he's getting out my things and bringing me in my night reading and all that kind of stuff. And I just thought about ordering all those kids in there. And what in the hell am I ordering him out there for?
<v Lyndon Johnson>今天早上我看着我的那个军士。他在那边有六个小孩。他帮我整理东西，带给我夜间读物之类的东西。我刚才就在想，我要命令所有那些孩子去那里。我到底是为了什么才命令他去那里的？

00:04:52.410 --> 00:04:52.660
<v Lyndon Johnson>[INTERPOSING VOICES]
<v Lyndon Johnson>[干扰声]

00:04:52.660 --> 00:05:10.960
<v Lyndon Johnson>What the hell is Vietnam worth to me? What is Laos worth to me? What is it worth to this country? Now, of course, if you start running the communists, they may just chase you right into your own kitchen.
<v Lyndon Johnson>越南对我来说到底有什么意义？老挝对我来说有什么意义？这对这个国家有什么意义？当然，如果你开始对付共产主义者，他们可能会把你一直追到你自己的厨房里。

00:05:10.960 --> 00:05:20.830
<v Mcgeorge Bundy>Yep, that's the trouble. And that is what the rest of that half of the world is going to think if this thing comes apart on us. That's the dilemma. That's exactly the dilemma.
<v Mcgeorge Bundy>是啊，这就是麻烦所在。如果这件事情在我们手上搞砸了，世界上另一半国家的人都会这么想。这就是两难境地。这正是两难境地。

00:05:20.830 --> 00:05:32.770
<v Lyndon Johnson>But everybody I talk to that's got any sense in there, they just says, "Oh, my god, please [UNINTELLIGIBLE]" But it's damned easy to get in a war, but it's going to be awfully hard to ever extricate yourself if you get in.
<v Lyndon Johnson>但我谈过的所有明白人都说：“哦，天哪，求你了[听不清]”。但卷入战争是再容易不过了，如果你陷进去，想脱身可就难上加难了。

00:05:32.770 --> 00:05:42.110
<v Mcgeorge Bundy>It's very easy. I'm very sensitive to the fact that people who are having trouble with--
<v Mcgeorge Bundy>非常容易。我非常敏感地意识到，那些在……方面有困难的人。

00:05:42.110 --> 00:06:06.340
<v Ira Glass>On the telephone, we are who we truly are, some of the time, anyway. Well, from WBEZ Chicago and Public Radio International, it's This American Life . I'm Ira Glass. Each week on our program, of course, we choose a theme, bring you a variety of different kinds of stories on that theme. Today's program, telephone stories, what we reveal about ourselves on the phone that we do not reveal any other way.
<v Ira Glass>在电话里，我们有时会表现出真实的一面。好了，来自芝加哥WBEZ和国际公共广播电台，这里是《美国生活》(This American Life)。我是艾拉·格拉斯(Ira Glass)。每周在我们的节目中，我们都会选择一个主题，带给你各种关于该主题的故事。今天的节目是电话故事，关于我们在电话里揭示出的、而在其他任何方式下都不会揭示的自我。

00:06:06.340 --> 00:06:52.410
<v Ira Glass>Act One, When the Wall Came Tumbling Down, the story of a teenager, illegal drug use, lying, stealing, and a kid's life completely changed when he heard how he sounded on the phone. Act Two, When Your Telephone is Your Medium. Sure, you can try to get your pop songs onto records, onto the radio, onto MTV. But what if your medium of choice is the telephone? We offer a case example, the band They Might Be Giants. Act Three, Telephone as History, moments from a normal human life saved on answering machine. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，当墙倒塌时，讲述了一个青少年的故事，涉及非法药物使用、撒谎、偷窃，以及一个孩子在听到自己在电话里的声音后，生活发生了彻底的改变。第二幕，当电话成为你的媒介。当然，你可以尝试把流行歌曲录成唱片，放到电台，放到MTV上。但如果你选择的媒介是电话呢？我们提供一个案例，乐队“他们可能是巨人”(They Might Be Giants)。第三幕，电话作为历史，记录在答录机里的普通人生活时刻。请继续收听。

00:06:52.410 --> 00:07:15.030
<v Ira Glass>Act One. Let's forget telephone numbers for a moment, and let's start with a zip code, 90210. That's where this story takes place, literally. Joshua was a student at Beverly Hills High School. Kids there had a lot of money, did a lot of drugs. And he started doing them, too, in a pretty serious way.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕。让我们暂时忘掉电话号码，从一个邮政编码开始，90210。这就是这个故事发生的地方，字面意义上的。约书亚(Joshua)是贝弗利山高中的一名学生。那里的孩子很有钱，吸毒成风。他也开始以一种相当严重的方式吸毒。

00:07:15.030 --> 00:07:39.620
<v Joshua>I failed English. I failed PE, even, which is difficult to do, unless you're running off getting stoned whenever you're supposed to be running around the track. And I just did whatever I wanted to do, whenever I wanted to do it.
<v Joshua>我英语不及格。我甚至连体育都不及格，这很难做到，除非你本该在操场上跑步时却跑去嗑药了。我只是想做什么就做什么，想什么时候做就什么时候做。

00:07:39.620 --> 00:08:16.160
<v Joshua>I stole money from my parents kind of regularly to finance my marijuana habit. I did break into my parents' tenants', basically, house a few times, mostly to steal drugs, not money. But that was the only stealing that I did. I never actually stole anything except for drugs and money. And the money, I only stole from my parents.
<v Joshua>我经常从父母那里偷钱来供我的大麻瘾。我基本上闯入过我父母的房客家里几次，主要是为了偷药，而不是钱。但那是我做的唯一的偷窃行为。我从来没偷过除了毒品和钱以外的任何东西。至于钱，我只从我父母那里偷。

00:08:16.160 --> 00:08:55.740
<v Joshua>Well, I noticed my dad started to punish me for things that I hadn't done, or at least things that he couldn't have possibly known that I had done. And I would have a great weekend planned. For instance, we were going to go out and do whatever sorts of craziness would come up. Maybe we'll go take some LSD or something like that, or mushrooms. And my dad would ground me the day before for no particular reason. And it was very strange. The timing was uncanny.
<v Joshua>嗯，我发现我爸爸开始因为我没做过的事情，或者至少是他不可能知道我做过的事情来惩罚我。我本计划好了一个很棒的周末。例如，我们打算出去做各种疯狂的事情。也许我们会去吃点LSD（迷幻药）之类的，或者蘑菇。而我爸爸在前一天无缘无故地禁我的足。这很奇怪。时间点诡异得令人不可思议。

00:08:55.740 --> 00:10:06.960
<v Joshua>And one day, he was driving me down to school, as he did every morning. And on the way to school, he told me that the administration of Beverly had contacted him and that they had narcs in the school that were posing as students. And they were going to run a sweep of the school and basically bust everyone who was involved in this. So he told me that the administration had contacted him because they knew that I was involved in this whole faction of bad eggs. And they also knew that I was not a dealer. Basically, they were calling him to warn him to basically give me a chance to get out of the bust. So he told me I'd better get it together because it's going to happen very soon.
<v Joshua>有一天，他像往常一样每天早上开车送我去学校。在去学校的路上，他告诉我，贝弗利的校方联系了他，说学校里有缉毒警察假扮成学生。他们打算在学校进行一次大搜查，基本上要把所有参与的人都抓起来。所以他告诉我，校方联系他是因为他们知道我参与了这一伙害群之马。他们也知道我不是毒贩。基本上，他们打电话给他是在警告他，好给我一个逃避抓捕的机会。所以他告诉我，最好收敛一点，因为很快就会发生。

00:10:06.960 --> 00:10:55.480
<v Joshua>And then, in the same conversation, he started naming all of the names of the kids that were involved. And they were names that there was no way he could know. And so the story was very credible the way it was laid out. And I believed it 100%. And I went to school that day, I remember, and of course, I told all of my friends that there was going to be this major bust happening at Beverly Hills High School very soon. And so everybody better quickly go undercover. And people became much more careful.
<v Joshua>然后，在同一个谈话中，他开始列举所有参与孩子的名字。那些名字是他不可能知道的。所以这个故事说得头头是道，非常可信。我百分之百相信了。我记得那天我去学校，当然，我告诉了我所有的朋友，贝弗利山高中很快会发生一次大搜查。所以大家最好赶快潜伏起来。人们变得谨慎多了。

00:10:55.480 --> 00:11:27.200
<v Joshua>About a month later, I was in the backyard. And there had been a windstorm the night before, a really big wind. And a panel on the side of the house had come off. And I was in the backyard with my friend. And we were smoking a joint like we always did. Every day after school, we would come home and smoke a joint. And my friend noticed this panel down.
<v Joshua>大约一个月后，我在后院。前一天晚上有一场风暴，风很大。房子侧面的一个嵌板掉了下来。我和我的朋友在后院。我们像往常一样在抽大麻烟。每天放学后，我们都会回家抽一支。我朋友注意到了这个掉下来的嵌板。

00:11:27.200 --> 00:12:05.160
<v Joshua>I'll never forget this one, actually. He said, "Dude, what is this? Come here, dude. And I'm standing on the other side of the backyard, smoking a joint, looking at the trees. And he's like, "Come here, dude." I walked over. He's like, "Look at this." And it didn't dawn on me. I looked at it, and I was like, "What is it? Strange piece of machinery inside of wall." It made no sense to me whatsoever. And he looked at me. He said, "Dude, your parents are taping your calls." And that meant that dad knew everything. No wonder he was grounding me at such crucial moments in my party life.
<v Joshua>说实话，我永远忘不了这一次。他说：“伙计，这是什么？过来，伙计。”我站在后院的另一边，抽着烟，看着树。他喊：“过来，伙计。”我走过去。他说：“看这个。”我还没反应过来。我看着它，心想：“这是什么？墙里有奇怪的机械。”我完全看不懂。他看着我说：“伙计，你父母在监听你的电话。”这意味着我爸知道了一切。难怪他在我派对生活的关键时刻给我禁足。

00:12:05.160 --> 00:12:11.810
<v Joshua>I'm getting some buds and some coke delivered here tonight.
<v Joshua>我今晚在这儿要弄点嫩芽（大麻）和可卡因。

00:12:11.810 --> 00:12:12.300
<v Boy>Really?
<v Boy>真的吗？

00:12:12.300 --> 00:12:21.740
<v Joshua>Yeah, so listen, if you guys want to come, just call me before you come. Well, you can come over here if you want.
<v Joshua>是啊，所以听着，如果你们想来，来之前先打电话给我。嗯，如果你们想的话，可以来我这儿。

00:12:21.740 --> 00:12:22.240
<v Boy>Really?
<v Boy>真的吗？

00:12:22.240 --> 00:12:34.830
<v Joshua>Yeah. Because they're going out, so--
<v Joshua>是啊。因为他们要出门了，所以——

00:12:34.830 --> 00:12:39.280
<v Joshua>You don't have five grams.
<v Joshua>你没有五克。

00:12:39.280 --> 00:12:40.730
<v Boy>Dude, what's the big deal?
<v Boy>伙计，这有什么大不了的？

00:12:40.730 --> 00:12:41.700
<v Joshua>I'm bumming hard.
<v Joshua>我非常沮丧。

00:12:41.700 --> 00:12:43.640
<v Boy>You're getting an eighth for $25 and a gram.
<v Boy>你花25美元能买到八分之一盎司再加一克。

00:12:43.640 --> 00:12:45.580
<v Joshua>All right, I guess I can--
<v Joshua>好吧，我想我可以——

00:12:45.580 --> 00:12:46.550
<v Boy>Is that cool?
<v Boy>这样行吗？

00:12:46.550 --> 00:12:48.850
<v Joshua>An eighth for $25 and a gram for $10?
<v Joshua>八分之一盎司25美元，一克10美元？

00:12:48.850 --> 00:12:49.330
<v Boy>Uh-huh.
<v Boy>嗯哼。

00:12:49.330 --> 00:12:54.630
<v Joshua>All right.
<v Joshua>好吧。

00:12:54.630 --> 00:13:08.870
<v Boy>The only weed he can get you is the same weed I can get you. But dude, it's [BLEEP].
<v Boy>他能给你的唯一大麻和我能给你的是一样的。但伙计，那是[脏话]。

00:13:08.870 --> 00:13:21.070
<v Joshua>When I saw the tape recorder, it was disbelief. I was in a state of disbelief. Pure shock.
<v Joshua>当我看到录音机时，我简直不敢相信。我处于一种难以置信的状态。纯粹的震惊。

00:13:21.070 --> 00:13:56.550
<v Joshua's Dad>This is what happened. My son was a very good student. All of a sudden, his grades started collapsing. His school things started collapsing. He started acting like a complete fool. Now, he's ready to graduate from Beverly High. And his attitude is becoming a total [BLEEP] box. He's becoming an asshole everywhere.
<v Joshua's Dad>事情是这样的。我儿子以前是个非常优秀的学生。突然间，他的成绩开始一落千丈。他的学校生活开始崩溃。他开始表现得像个十足的傻瓜。现在，他准备从贝弗利高中毕业了。他的态度变得像个彻底的[脏话]。他变得到处都是混蛋。

00:13:56.550 --> 00:14:17.190
<v Joshua's Dad>So I thought, you know, the only way I can figure out what's happening with him is-- because he comes home every day and he's alone a long time at home, got a lot of time on the phone. Right? So I figure, well, what I'll do is that I'll tape record his phone calls.
<v Joshua's Dad>所以我想，你知道，我能弄清楚他发生了什么事的唯一方法是——因为他每天回家后都有很长一段时间独自在家，他在电话上花了很多时间。对吧？所以我心想，好吧，我要做的就是录下他的电话。

