<v Joshua>It was such a strange sort of accusation I was making, once again, because simultaneously, I was admitting to him that I knew that he knew everything that was going on. So on the one hand, I'm saying, "Dad, how could you be so horrible as to invade my privacy like this?" while simultaneously realizing that he knew that I had been stealing money from him to support my pot habits. And he said, "You're right, I'm taping your calls. I've been doing it for quite a while, as a matter of fact. And I'm not going to do it anymore. I'm not going to tape your telephone calls anymore." He said, "There's something you don't know yet, but I'll tell you now. And perhaps you will not understand what I'm saying. But you think that because I'm your father and I'm in this role of the disciplinarian, that it's between you and I. What you haven't realized yet is that your actions have far more impact on your own life than they will on mine. I've already made my life. You have to make your life. And you don't know it yet." He said, "I'm going to take the tape recorder out tomorrow."
<v Joshua>这是一种很奇怪的指责，再一次，因为与此同时，我向他承认我知道他知道正在发生的一切。所以一方面，我说：“爸，你怎么能这么恶劣，这样侵犯我的隐私？”同时意识到他知道我一直偷他的钱来维持我的大麻瘾。他说：“你说得对，我在录你的电话。事实上，我已经录了有一段时间了。我不会再这样做了。我不会再录你的电话了。”他说：“有件事你还不知道，但我现在告诉你。也许你不会明白我在说什么。但你认为因为我是你的父亲，我扮演的是纪律管教者的角色，所以这是你我之间的问题。你还没意识到的是，你的行为对你自己生活的影响要远大于对我的影响。我已经过完了我的生活。你必须创造你的生活。而你还不知道这一点。”他说：“我明天会把录音机拿走。我只想要你做一件事。”我说：“什么？”他说：“我大概有40盘关于你的90分钟录音带。我要把它们给你。我希望你听听它们。这就是我的全部要求。没有惩罚。这一切结束了。这一章结束了。但这些磁带现在属于你了。”

00:26:57.610 --> 00:27:47.260
<v Joshua>And I was waiting for the punishment at this point, because there must be some amazing punishment coming down. Because he knew everything. Military school, or what's he going to do? He said, "I'll take the tape recorder out tomorrow. And there's only one thing I want you to do." And I said, "What?" And he said, "I have about 40 tapes, about 40 90-minute tapes of you. I'm going to give them to you. And I want you to listen to them. And that's all I ask. No punishment. This is over. This chapter is over. But the tapes are now yours."
<v Joshua>我当时在等着惩罚，因为肯定会有某种惊人的惩罚降临。因为他什么都知道。军校，或者他打算怎么做？他说：“我明天会把录音机拿走。我只想要你做一件事。”我说：“什么？”他说：“我大概有40盘关于你的90分钟录音带。我要把它们给你。我希望你听听它们。这就是我的全部要求。没有惩罚。这一切结束了。这一章结束了。但这些磁带现在属于你了。”

00:27:47.260 --> 00:27:50.730
<v Joshua>[DIAL TONE]
<v Joshua>[拨号音]

00:27:50.730 --> 00:27:51.650
<v Joshua>Hello?
<v Joshua>喂？

00:27:51.650 --> 00:27:52.990
<v Girl>Hi. What's the matter?
<v Girl>嗨。怎么了？

00:27:52.990 --> 00:27:56.260
<v Joshua>Nothing.
<v Joshua>没什么。

00:27:56.260 --> 00:27:58.870
<v Girl>All right, well, you don't have to talk.
<v Girl>好吧，你没必要非得说话。

00:27:58.870 --> 00:28:06.250
<v Joshua>It was embarrassing. I was embarrassing myself, listening to myself.
<v Joshua>那很尴尬。我在听自己的时候，感到自己很丢脸。

00:28:06.250 --> 00:28:07.760
<v Girl>Can I ask you something?
<v Girl>我可以问你一件事吗？

00:28:07.760 --> 00:28:08.940
<v Joshua>What's that?
<v Joshua>什么事？

00:28:08.940 --> 00:28:12.490
<v Girl>Are you awake enough to answer?
<v Girl>你醒着能回答吗？

00:28:12.490 --> 00:28:20.490
<v Joshua>Yeah, I think I can, if I try real hard.
<v Joshua>是啊，我想如果我努力尝试的话，我可以。

00:28:20.490 --> 00:28:26.710
<v Girl>I get the feeling that you've changed the way you feel about me. And I don't like that.
<v Girl>我感觉你对我改变了看法。我不喜欢那样。

00:28:26.710 --> 00:28:28.060
<v Joshua>Oh, really?
<v Joshua>哦，真的吗？

00:28:28.060 --> 00:28:34.050
<v Girl>I mean, obviously, there's nothing I can do.
<v Girl>我的意思是，显然，我什么都做不了。

00:28:34.050 --> 00:28:36.490
<v Joshua>Nah, nah, nah.
<v Joshua>不，不，不。

00:28:36.490 --> 00:28:38.640
<v Girl>Nah, nah, what?
<v Girl>不，不，什么？

00:28:38.640 --> 00:28:42.460
<v Joshua>I don't know what you're talking about.
<v Joshua>我不知道你在说什么。

00:28:42.460 --> 00:28:44.860
<v Girl>Well, then, how come you haven't called me in so long?
<v Girl>那，为什么你这么久没给我打电话了？

00:28:44.860 --> 00:28:49.140
<v Joshua>I've been busy.
<v Joshua>我一直很忙。

00:28:49.140 --> 00:28:52.190
<v Girl>Well, that's really the truth?
<v Girl>那真的是实话吗？

00:28:52.190 --> 00:28:54.800
<v Joshua>Yeah, I hope so.
<v Joshua>是啊，我希望是。

00:28:54.800 --> 00:29:22.410
<v Joshua>It was about a week afterwards, and I couldn't really listen to them. I had a very hard time listening to them. In a way, it was kind of surrealistic because I had no idea what I sounded like. And I didn't like what I sounded like at all. I thought I sounded really kind of unkind, self-serving, mean, basically, and totally uncaring.
<v Joshua>那大约是一周后，我真的听不下去。我听它们听得很吃力。某种程度上，这有点超现实，因为我根本不知道自己的声音听起来是什么样。我一点也不喜欢自己的声音。我觉得自己听起来真的很刻薄、自私、卑鄙，而且完全不关心别人。

00:29:22.410 --> 00:29:23.150
<v Joshua>Hello?
<v Joshua>喂？

00:29:23.150 --> 00:29:23.620
<v Boy>Hey, dude.
<v Boy>嘿，伙计。

00:29:23.620 --> 00:29:24.570
<v Joshua>Hey.
<v Joshua>嘿。

00:29:24.570 --> 00:29:25.510
<v Boy>What's happening?
<v Boy>在干嘛呢？

00:29:25.510 --> 00:29:25.960
<v Joshua>Not much.
<v Joshua>没干嘛。

00:29:25.960 --> 00:29:27.310
<v Boy>Do you have any finals tomorrow?
<v Boy>你明天有期末考试吗？

00:29:27.310 --> 00:29:27.770
<v Joshua>Yeah.
<v Joshua>有。

00:29:27.770 --> 00:29:28.240
<v Boy>What?
<v Boy>什么？

00:29:28.240 --> 00:29:29.160
<v Joshua>English.
<v Joshua>英语。

00:29:29.160 --> 00:29:29.630
<v Boy>Really?
<v Boy>真的吗？

00:29:29.630 --> 00:29:30.090
<v Joshua>Yeah.
<v Joshua>嗯。

00:29:30.090 --> 00:29:31.940
<v Boy>I still don't have any.
<v Boy>我还一个都没有呢。

00:29:31.940 --> 00:29:33.350
<v Joshua>I'm kind of busy right now.
<v Joshua>我现在有点忙。

00:29:33.350 --> 00:29:36.590
<v Boy>I can tell. It sounds like people are in the background.
<v Boy>我看得出来。听起来背景里有人。

00:29:36.590 --> 00:29:37.910
<v Joshua>They are.
<v Joshua>是有人。

00:29:37.910 --> 00:29:39.240
<v Boy>Parental units?
<v Boy>父母单位的人？

00:29:39.240 --> 00:29:39.650
<v Joshua>Huh?
<v Joshua>哈？

00:29:39.650 --> 00:29:40.910
<v Boy>Parental units?
<v Boy>父母单位的人？

00:29:40.910 --> 00:29:43.110
<v Joshua>No, no, no. Friend units.
<v Joshua>不，不，不。朋友单位的。

00:29:43.110 --> 00:29:44.560
<v Boy>Oh, that sounds radical.
<v Boy>哦，听起来很激进。

00:29:44.560 --> 00:29:45.530
<v Joshua>Yeah, it is. Bye.
<v Joshua>是啊，是挺激进的。拜。

00:29:45.530 --> 00:29:46.990
<v Boy>Great.
<v Boy>太棒了。

00:29:46.990 --> 00:29:47.470
<v Boy>[DIAL TONE]
<v Boy>[拨号音]

00:29:47.470 --> 00:30:12.130
<v Joshua>I was very self-centered and egotistical and uncaring of other people. It was about me. I was the star of my own stage. And everybody else could basically go to hell, as far as I was concerned. I had never realized that aspect of my personality. I didn't know how mean, in that sense, I had gotten.
<v Joshua>我是个非常以自我为中心、利己、且不关心别人的人。一切都是关于我。我是我自己舞台上的明星。至于其他人，只要我关心，他们基本上可以去死。我从未意识到我人格中的那一面。我不知道在那方面我变得有多刻薄。

00:30:12.130 --> 00:30:13.110
<v Joshua>Hello?
<v Joshua>喂？

00:30:13.110 --> 00:30:16.880
<v Man>Hello, is it possible for me to speak with Mr. Or Mrs. O'Hara?
<v Man>喂，请问我可以和奥哈拉先生或太太通话吗？

00:30:16.880 --> 00:30:18.970
<v Joshua>No.
<v Joshua>不能。

00:30:18.970 --> 00:30:20.270
<v Joshua>Hello?
<v Joshua>喂？

00:30:20.270 --> 00:30:22.270
<v Man>May I speak with  Novella?
<v Man>我可以和诺薇拉(Novella)通话吗？

00:30:22.270 --> 00:30:23.080
<v Joshua>What?
<v Joshua>什么？

00:30:23.080 --> 00:30:24.070
<v Man>Novella.
<v Man>诺薇拉。

00:30:24.070 --> 00:30:25.060
<v Joshua>Who is this?
<v Joshua>你是哪位？

00:30:25.060 --> 00:30:28.860
<v Man>My name is Crawford. I'm with the VFW.
<v Man>我叫克劳福德(Crawford)。我是海外战争退伍军人协会(VFW)的。

00:30:28.860 --> 00:30:31.300
<v Joshua>What is it in regards to?
<v Joshua>请问有什么事？

00:30:31.300 --> 00:30:35.970
<v Man>The National Veteran Wheelchair Games.
<v Man>全国退伍军人轮椅运动会。

00:30:35.970 --> 00:30:39.820
<v Joshua>Well, can I take a message for her?
<v Joshua>那，我可以帮她带个口信吗？

00:30:39.820 --> 00:30:42.000
<v Man>No, I'll call back later.
<v Man>不，我晚点再打来。

00:30:42.000 --> 00:30:43.860
<v Joshua>Well, listen. Hello?
<v Joshua>那，听着。喂？

00:30:43.860 --> 00:30:44.280
<v Man>Yes?
<v Man>喂？

00:30:44.280 --> 00:30:46.240
<v Joshua>She's dead.
<v Joshua>她死了。

00:30:46.240 --> 00:30:47.610
<v Man>Oh, I see.
<v Man>哦，明白了。

00:30:47.610 --> 00:30:52.310
<v Joshua>So, you know, I think-- What is it in regards to? Do you owe us some money?
<v Joshua>所以，你知道，我想——请问有什么事？你欠我们钱吗？

00:30:52.310 --> 00:30:57.340
<v Man>No, we were selling and delivering heavy-duty plastic trash bags to raise funds.
<v Man>不，我们在卖和运送重型塑料垃圾袋以筹集资金。

00:30:57.340 --> 00:31:05.490
<v Joshua>So you wanted money.
<v Joshua>所以你是要钱。

00:31:05.490 --> 00:31:33.700
<v Joshua>And I remember listening to that on the tapes subsequently. And again, it was just like, my god, I'm a monster. This is outrageous. Look at the way I'm treating people. Because I don't feel that way, and I'm not that type of person. But at the time, I was, very much. And I didn't know it. I didn't know how little I valued friendship or other people's feelings.
<v Joshua>我记得后来在录音带里听到了那一幕。我再一次感到，我的天，我是个怪物。这太离谱了。看看我对待别人的方式。因为我不觉得那是我，我不是那种人。但在当时，我的确就是那样。我不知道这一点。我不知道我有多么轻视友谊或他人的感受。

00:31:33.700 --> 00:31:42.080
<v Boy>Then we went to this dance club, and we just met some people. There was these girls. But we weren't going to scam off them. We were just friends.
<v Boy>然后我们去了这家舞厅，我们就认识了一些人。有这些女孩。但我们不是要去骗她们。我们只是朋友。

00:31:42.080 --> 00:31:42.960
<v Joshua>Uh-huh.
<v Joshua>嗯哼。

00:31:42.960 --> 00:31:45.290
<v Boy>We waited for like a half hour to get our table--
<v Boy>我们等了大约半小时才得到我们的桌子——

00:31:45.290 --> 00:31:46.750
<v Joshua>And did you scam off them?
<v Joshua>那你们骗了她们吗？

00:31:46.750 --> 00:31:46.950
<v Boy>What?
<v Boy>什么？

00:31:46.950 --> 00:31:48.130
<v Joshua>Did you scam them? Or no?
<v Joshua>你们骗她们了吗？还是没有？

00:31:48.130 --> 00:31:48.940
<v Boy>No.
<v Boy>没有。

00:31:48.940 --> 00:31:50.610
<v Joshua>They were just nice?
<v Joshua>她们只是很好？

00:31:50.610 --> 00:31:56.320
<v Boy>Yeah. They were just like-- It was just conversation. It was--
<v Boy>是啊。她们只是……这只是对话。这只是——

00:31:56.320 --> 00:32:10.580
<v Joshua>Bleh. Dude, if I was there, that wouldn't have happened. We would've done some [BLEEP].
<v Joshua>呸。伙计，如果我在那儿，那就不会发生。我们会做些[脏话]。

00:32:10.580 --> 00:32:52.790
<v Joshua>It took me about five or six years before I managed to listen to all of them. My course of life changed dramatically because of that event. And a lot changed. I still partied. But I put it back, as far as the importance, where it was on the hierarchy. It was like smoking pot ceased to be number one. And lying, deceiving, cheating, stealing, all of that, I just stopped. Stopped it all.
<v Joshua>我花了大约五六年时间才把它们听完。我的生活轨迹因为那件事发生了巨大的改变。很多事情都变了。我还是去派对。但我把它的重要性放回了它在层次结构中应有的位置。就像吸食大麻不再是头等大事了。撒谎、欺骗、作弊、偷窃，所有这些，我全都停了。全部停了。

00:32:52.790 --> 00:33:04.830
<v Joshua's Dad>It was amazing. He understood the entire thing that he was doing that was so-- and believe me, it was stupid.
<v Joshua's Dad>这太神奇了。他理解了他所做的那整件事，那真的很……相信我，真的很愚蠢。

00:33:04.830 --> 00:33:29.410
<v Joshua>Because I never had the opportunity to hear myself. I didn't know. I didn't know what I was doing. The tone of voice of my quote-quote "friends"-- I could hear in their voice on the tapes, pain because of my actions and my uncaring. That was an awakening.
<v Joshua>因为我从来没有机会听到我自己。我不知道。我不知道我在做什么。那些所谓的“朋友”的语调——我能从磁带里的声音中听到因为我的行为和漠不关心而产生的痛苦。那是一次觉醒。

00:33:29.410 --> 00:33:32.510
<v Girl>I haven't heard from you for a while. Ouch.
<v Girl>我已经很久没听到你的消息了。哎哟。

00:33:32.510 --> 00:33:34.100
<v Joshua>I haven't heard from you for a while.
<v Joshua>我已经很久没听到你的消息了。

00:33:34.100 --> 00:33:35.740
<v Girl>Yes, you have.
<v Girl>不对，你听到了。

00:33:35.740 --> 00:33:36.780
<v Joshua>Oh yeah, when?
<v Joshua>哦，是吗，什么时候？

00:33:36.780 --> 00:33:39.660
<v Girl>Last time we talked, I called you.
<v Girl>上次我们通话时，我打给了你。

00:33:39.660 --> 00:33:44.240
<v Joshua>Oh, you mean you actually keep track?
<v Joshua>哦，你是说你真的有在记录？

00:33:44.240 --> 00:33:47.820
<v Girl>No, I just remember that.
<v Girl>不，我只是记得那个。

00:33:47.820 --> 00:33:48.820
<v Joshua>Really?
<v Joshua>真的吗？

00:33:48.820 --> 00:34:16.540
<v Joshua>I became the watcher of myself while I was acting simultaneously. And I still do that to this day. I see, I'm intensely aware of my effect on other people. That's one thing that definitely came out. It's like I know what other people are feeling about me.
<v Joshua>我在行动的同时成为了自己的观察者。时至今日我依然这样做。我看到了，我非常清楚自己对他人的影响。这是绝对产生的影响之一。就像我知道别人对我有什么感觉。

00:34:16.540 --> 00:34:20.730
<v Joshua>That was an interesting look you gave me today.
<v Joshua>你今天看我的那个眼神很有意思。

00:34:20.730 --> 00:34:23.290
<v Girl>Oh. Ha. I know.
<v Girl>哦。哈。我知道。

00:34:23.290 --> 00:34:27.830
<v Joshua>So what's the problem with you? What are you pissed off at me for?
<v Joshua>所以你有什么问题？你因为什么生我的气？

00:34:27.830 --> 00:34:30.440
<v Girl>I don't know.
<v Girl>我不知道。

00:34:30.440 --> 00:34:32.400
<v Joshua>Is is that time of the month again?
<v Joshua>是不是又到了每个月的那几天？

00:34:32.400 --> 00:34:35.850
<v Girl>No. [INAUDIBLE]
<v Girl>不是。[听不清]

00:34:35.850 --> 00:34:39.570
<v Joshua>Well, then, what's wrong?
<v Joshua>那，怎么了？

00:34:39.570 --> 00:34:49.640
<v Girl>Well, I guess the basic thing is I don't like your fluctuation in attention towards me.
<v Girl>嗯，我想最基本的事情是我不喜欢你对我的关注度忽高忽低。

00:34:49.640 --> 00:35:07.000
<v Joshua>What are you talking about? If I'm not going somewhere, if I have not got a set place that I am off to-- and I'm probably usually late-- then I stop and talk to you.
<v Joshua>你在说什么？如果我不是要去某个地方，如果我没有设定好我要去的地方——而且我通常总是迟到——那我就会停下来和你聊天。

00:35:07.000 --> 00:35:07.830
<v Girl>Uh-huh.
<v Girl>嗯哼。

00:35:07.830 --> 00:35:12.450
<v Joshua>And well, forget it then. [BLEEP].
<v Joshua>那好吧，算了。[脏话]。

00:35:12.450 --> 00:35:16.370
<v Girl>Wait. Hold on. Mom?
<v Girl>等等。等一下。妈？

00:35:16.370 --> 00:35:19.330
<v Girl>[DIAL TONE]
<v Girl>[拨号音]

00:35:19.330 --> 00:36:02.240
<v Joshua>It was valuable, very valuable, to be able to witness myself in that way, although painful. I got to see myself. It's a rare gift, in a way, to be able to see yourself from the outside, from an objective point of view. I think it's probably always going to be difficult to watch yourself. Given an opportunity, I think most people would probably not want to see themselves that clearly.
<v Joshua>能够以那种方式见证自己非常有价值，虽然很痛苦。我得以看见自己。从某种意义上说，能从外部、从客观的角度看待自己，是一种罕见的礼物。我想注视自己可能永远是件困难的事。如果有机会，我想大多数人可能不想把自己看得那么清楚。

00:36:02.240 --> 00:36:06.450
<v Ira Glass>Joshua and his father talked to Dave Kestenbaum, who's a writer for Science magazine, and to Julie Snyder.
<v Ira Glass>约书亚和他父亲接受了《科学》杂志撰稿人戴夫·凯斯滕鲍姆(Dave Kestenbaum)和朱莉·斯奈德(Julie Snyder)的采访。

00:36:06.450 --> 00:36:37.040
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "I'VE GOT A NEW HOME" BY PILGRIM TRAVELERS]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - “我有一个新家”，朝圣者旅行者乐队]

00:36:37.040 --> 00:37:49.390
<v Ira Glass>Coming up, telephone as an artistic medium, telephone as muse. What a songwriter can learn from the telephone. That's in a minute, from Public Radio International, when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>接下来，电话作为艺术媒介，电话作为缪斯。词曲作家能从电话里学到什么。一分钟后，来自国际公共广播电台，节目继续。

00:37:49.390 --> 00:38:09.500
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life . I'm Ira Glass. Each week on our program, of course, we choose a theme, invite a variety of reporters and writers and performers to tackle that theme. Today's show, things we learn on the phone, who we are on the phone, things we find on the phone that we can not find anywhere else. We have arrived at Act Two of our program, When the Telephone is Your Medium.
<v Ira Glass>这里是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。每周在我们的节目中，我们都会选择一个主题，邀请各种记者、作家和表演者来探讨这个主题。今天的节目，我们在电话里学到的东西，我们在电话里的样子，我们在电话里找到的而在其他地方找不到的东西。我们已经进入了节目的第二幕，当电话成为你的媒介。

00:38:09.500 --> 00:38:32.330
<v Ira Glass>Well, the band They Might Be Giants do not have a song on every episode of This American Life . But I have to say, most weeks, at some point, contributing editor Sarah Vowell suggests a song of theirs that might work in the show. And that's because they are not only prolific. More than other bands, they write about an astonishing number of things in this surprising, funny way.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，乐队“他们可能是巨人”并没有在《美国生活》的每一集里都有一首歌。但我必须说，大多数周，在某个时候，特约编辑莎拉·沃威尔(Sarah Vowell)都会建议一首他们的歌，觉得可能适合节目。这是因为他们不仅多产，而且比其他乐队更以令人惊奇的、有趣的方式撰写数量惊人的事物。

00:38:32.330 --> 00:38:54.450
<v Ira Glass>Also, although they are an actual pop band with actual pop hits, it is hard to think of any band in the last 20 years with more inventive arrangements. They just have this sound, this sense of how different instruments go together. They bring pre-rock instruments into the rock universe in this completely charming way. John Linnell of They Might Be Giants, he is the Keith Richards of the bass clarinet.
<v Ira Glass>此外，虽然他们是一支真正的流行乐队，有着真正的流行热曲，但很难想到过去20年里有哪支乐队拥有比他们更具创造性的编曲。他们就是有这种声音，这种对不同乐器如何搭配在一起的感知。他们以一种完全迷人的方式将前摇滚时代的乐器带入了摇滚宇宙。“他们可能是巨人”的约翰·林内尔(John Linnell)，他是低音单簧管界的基思·理查兹(Keith Richards)。

00:38:54.450 --> 00:39:12.940
<v Ira Glass>And at the heart of their artistic practices-- you know we have a theme to the show every week. We have a theme to this show. And you know what the theme is. So you know where I'm going with this-- At the heart of their artistic practice is the telephone. It was, anyway. Still sort of is. Sarah Vowell visited them in Brooklyn.
<v Ira Glass>在他们艺术实践的核心——你知道我们每周节目都有一个主题。我们有这个节目的主题。你知道主题是什么。所以你明白我的意思了——他们艺术实践的核心是电话。反正曾经是。现在某种程度上依然是。莎拉·沃威尔在布鲁克林拜访了他们。

00:39:12.940 --> 00:39:22.450
<v Sarah Vowell>What if art cost a quarter a pop? What if it offered home delivery? What if it was short, but sweet? What if art was just a phone call away?
<v Sarah Vowell>如果艺术每次只需25美分呢？如果它提供送货上门呢？如果它简短而甜蜜呢？如果艺术只需一个电话就能触及呢？

00:39:22.450 --> 00:39:27.880
<v Sarah Vowell>[TELEPHONE RINGING]
<v Sarah Vowell>[电话铃声]

00:39:27.880 --> 00:39:38.300
<v John Linnell>Hello, everybody. This is John of They Might Be Giants. Thank you for calling our Dial-a-Song service. We hope you like it, hope it sounds good over your phone. Thank you for calling. Call back anytime.
<v John Linnell>大家好。这里是“他们可能是巨人”的约翰。感谢您致电我们的“拨号歌曲”(Dial-a-Song)服务。希望您喜欢，希望它在您的电话里听起来不错。感谢您的致电。随时再打来。

00:39:38.300 --> 00:39:57.330
<v John Linnell>[MUSIC PLAYING - "DR. WORM" BY THEY MIGHT BE GIANTS]
<v John Linnell>[音乐 - “虫子博士”(Dr. Worm)，他们可能是巨人]

00:39:57.330 --> 00:40:16.780
<v Sarah Vowell>It's a simple, beautiful idea, that anyone, anywhere, anytime, can phone a number in Brooklyn, listen to one of They Might Be Giants' wonderful or wonderfully weird songs, and it doesn't cost anything more than any other normal call. Dial-a-Song's motto, "Free, when you call from work."
<v Sarah Vowell>这是一个简单而美妙的想法，任何人，在任何地方，任何时间，都可以拨打布鲁克林的号码，聆听“他们可能是巨人”的精彩或古怪的歌曲，而且费用并不比任何其他普通电话高。“拨号歌曲”的座右铭是：“从公司打来是免费的。”

00:40:16.780 --> 00:40:17.690
<v Sarah Vowell>[TELEPHONE RINGING]
<v Sarah Vowell>[电话铃声]

00:40:17.690 --> 00:40:30.820
<v Sarah Vowell>[MUSIC PLAYING]
<v Sarah Vowell>[音乐播放]

00:40:30.820 --> 00:40:52.420
<v Ira Glass>When John Flansberg and John Linnell of They Might Be Giants started their Dial-a-Song service 15 years ago, they didn't have a record contract. All they had was an answering machine and a dream. And that dream was to get people besides their friends to hear their music. Then they got a record deal. They were on the radio, on MTV, even.
<v Ira Glass>当约翰·弗兰斯伯格(John Flansberg)和约翰·林内尔(John Linnell)在15年前开启他们的“拨号歌曲”服务时，他们并没有唱片合约。他们只有一台答录机和一个梦想。那个梦想是让朋友之外的人听到他们的音乐。后来他们签了唱片合约。他们上了电台，甚至上了MTV。

00:40:52.420 --> 00:41:26.480
<v Ira Glass>They considered dismantling the democratic empire of Dial-a-Song. But they were afraid their fans would accuse them of selling out, of turning their back on the people-- and by the people, I mean the people. And you can understand that the first year, the first five years, even. But it's been 15 years now. This funny little idea that they had back during the early Reagan administration, now, it's just who they are. Ladies and gentlemen, they rock the stage, they rock the records, they rock the phone lines.
<v Ira Glass>他们考虑过拆除“拨号歌曲”这个民主帝国。但他们担心粉丝会指责他们为了赚钱而变节，背叛了人民——我所说的人民，是指大众。你可以理解前五年，甚至前五年。但这已经是15年了。这个他们在里根政府早期想出的有趣的小点子，现在，这只是他们本身的样子。女士们，先生们，他们震撼舞台，他们震撼唱片，他们震撼电话线。

00:41:26.480 --> 00:41:28.790
<v Sarah Vowell>Do you remember the first song, the first Dial-a-Song song?
<v Sarah Vowell>你还记得第一首歌，第一首“拨号歌曲”吗？

00:41:28.790 --> 00:41:48.510
<v John Linnell>I do. Because John said, I set it up. I was living in a different part of Brooklyn, so I phoned him up. And it was "Toddler Hiway." And he recorded it really quietly because we had this problem with the phone machine, where a loud sound with this particular model would actually tell the machine that that was the end of the message.
<v John Linnell>我记得。因为约翰说，我把它设置好了。我当时住在布鲁克林的另一个地方，所以我给他打了电话。那是“幼儿高速公路”(Toddler Hiway)。他录得很轻，因为我们的电话机有问题，这种型号如果声音太大，实际上会告诉机器那是留言的结尾。

00:41:48.510 --> 00:41:50.090
<v John Flansberg>It would think it was the beep.
<v John Flansberg>它会以为那是哔哔声。

00:41:50.090 --> 00:41:57.550
<v John Linnell>It would think it was the beep. So almost all our songs had to be kind of tamed. And that particular note was completely off-limits.
<v John Linnell>它会以为那是哔哔声。所以我们几乎所有的歌都必须经过某种驯服。那个特定的音符是完全禁止的。

00:41:57.550 --> 00:42:15.930
<v John Flansberg>Yeah, anytime there was a long note sung, we would have to break it up. We would actually sort of pop it out of the mix. So it would just be like, "I've been ah-ah-ah-ah-ah-ah," to kind of keep it from just returning.
<v John Flansberg>是啊，任何时候有长音，我们都不得不把它切断。我们实际上会把它从混音中弹出。所以它听起来就像，“我一直啊-啊-啊-啊-啊-啊，”以此防止它直接挂断。

00:42:15.930 --> 00:42:39.330
<v Sarah Vowell>Sure, there are some adjustments you have to make if the telephone is the medium through which you express your art. But there are certain advantages to this system. You can monitor individual audience response in a way that's unavailable to more successful recording artists who restrict themselves to concerts and radio and TV-- you know, media where more than one person at a time can hear the song.
<v Sarah Vowell>当然，如果电话是你表达艺术的媒介，你确实需要做一些调整。但这个系统有一些特定的优势。你可以以一种受限制于只在音乐会、广播和电视上——你知道，那种一次可以多人同时听歌的媒介——的更成功录音艺术家所无法企及的方式监控个人听众的反应。

00:42:39.330 --> 00:42:58.720
<v John Flansberg>Well, I think up until Dial-a-Song, we were the kind of guys who had a four-track recorder in their bedroom. And we would just overdub our voices or our instruments 5 million times, and go like, "Oh, listen to what the guitar sounds like if it's been overdubbed 5,000 times." And it would just kind of be, wahhhhhh. "Oh, that sounds really cool."
<v John Flansberg>嗯，我想在“拨号歌曲”之前，我们是那种卧室里有四轨录音机的人。我们会给我们的声音或乐器叠加500万次，然后说，“哦，听听如果吉他叠加5000次听起来是什么样。”它就会像这样，哇……“哦，那听起来真的很酷。”

00:42:58.720 --> 00:43:18.620
<v John Flansberg>And then, doing Dial-a-Song actually made us realize, you're playing the song, people hang up in the middle, you can tell right away if they don't like-- you just sort of play your 5,000 guitar symphony over the thing. And two seconds into it, it's like, click. And you know that they hated it. So it really was-- it was definitely like rock school.
<v John Flansberg>然后，做“拨号歌曲”实际上让我们意识到，你正在播放歌曲，人们在中间挂断了，你可以立刻看出他们是否不喜欢——你只是在播放你的5000吉他交响曲。两秒钟后，就发出咔哒声。你知道他们讨厌它。所以它确实是——它绝对就像摇滚学校。

00:43:18.620 --> 00:43:35.340
<v John Linnell>It definitely moved us in a particular direction of really clarifying what we were doing, and also clarifying the arrangement, making it really simple sounding so that you could hear what was going on over the phone.
<v John Linnell>它绝对让我们朝着真正理清我们正在做的事情的方向发展，也理清了编曲，让它听起来非常简单，这样你就能通过电话听到正在发生什么。

00:43:35.340 --> 00:43:56.990
<v Sarah Vowell>Right now, somewhere in this nation, there's a music student spending thousands of dollars to learn a discipline that he might master more quickly if he'd just invest in a decent voicemail system.
<v Sarah Vowell>现在，在这个国家的某个地方，有一个音乐专业的学生花着成千上万的钱去学习一门学科，如果他只是投资一个像样的语音信箱系统，他可能会学得更快。

00:43:56.990 --> 00:44:14.960
<v Sarah Vowell>Has it changed your ideas about where art comes from? Because I was reading that New Yorker article on Frank Sinatra. And there's this part where Ava Gardner runs out on him and breaks his heart. And he helps write "I'm a Fool to Want You." And then he stumbles into the recording studio and does it in one take and stumbles out in tears.
<v Sarah Vowell>这有没有改变你对艺术来源的想法？因为我正在读那篇关于弗兰克·辛纳屈(Frank Sinatra)的《纽约客》文章。其中有一段是艾娃·加德纳(Ava Gardner)离开了他，伤透了他的心。然后他参与创作了《为你而痴》(I'm a Fool to Want You)。然后他跌跌撞撞地走进录音室，一次录制完成，又哭着跌跌撞撞地走出来。

00:44:14.960 --> 00:44:18.310
<v John Linnell>But he actually always stumbled in and out of the studio.
<v John Linnell>但他实际上总是跌跌撞撞地进出录音室。

00:44:18.310 --> 00:45:08.280
<v John Flansberg>But also, if you talk to Billy May or talk to the people who put the arrangement together or the person who wrote the song, they might disagree about where the greatness of any given song comes from. They're involved in the process, too. A lot of people like to think of inspiration as being, you're lying on the side of a hill, and you're waiting for the clouds to cover and then the lightning to strike you. It's this really elaborate, long process of kind of waiting for it to happen. And sometimes, I think you can jump into the sky, grab the cloud, and grab the lightning yourself.
<v John Flansberg>但同样，如果你和比利·梅(Billy May)交谈，或者和那些整理编曲的人，或者写歌的人交谈，他们可能对某一首歌的伟大之处来自哪里有不同意见。他们也参与了这个过程。很多人喜欢把灵感想象成，你躺在山坡上，等待云层覆盖，然后闪电击中你。这是一个非常精致、漫长的过程，等待它发生。有时，我认为你可以跳向天空，自己抓住云，抓住闪电。

00:45:08.280 --> 00:45:27.680
<v Sarah Vowell>Many, perhaps most, of the tunes on Dial-a-Song won't end up on a They Might Be Giants album. But all the songs on the band's albums have been previewed on Dial-a-Song, which means that their fans are in on their process. Here's how one of the band's best known singles, "Don't Let's Start," sounded in its initial Dial-a-Song form.
<v Sarah Vowell>许多，也许是大多数，“拨号歌曲”上的曲子最终不会出现在“他们可能是巨人”的专辑中。但乐队专辑上的所有歌曲都曾在“拨号歌曲”上试听过，这意味着他们的粉丝参与了他们的过程。这是乐队最著名的单曲之一，“不要开始”(Don't Let's Start)，在最初的“拨号歌曲”形式下听起来的样子。

00:45:27.680 --> 00:45:55.870
<v Sarah Vowell>[MUSIC PLAYING - "DON'T LET'S START" BY THEY MIGHT BE GIANTS]
<v Sarah Vowell>[音乐 - “不要开始”，他们可能是巨人]

00:45:55.870 --> 00:46:01.030
<v Ira Glass>And this is the final, more compelling rock and roll "Don't Let's Start" that ended up on the album.
<v Ira Glass>这是最终出现在专辑中、更引人注目的摇滚版“不要开始”。

00:46:01.030 --> 00:46:25.540
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC PLAYING - "DON'T LET'S START" BY THEY MIGHT BE GIANTS]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - “不要开始”，他们可能是巨人]

00:46:25.540 --> 00:46:31.800
<v Ira Glass>The best lyrics in the song are ones they added over what had been just an instrumental bridge in the Dial-a-Song version.
<v Ira Glass>这首歌里最好的歌词是他们在一个只是器乐过门的“拨号歌曲”版本上添加的。

00:46:31.800 --> 00:46:47.590
<v They Might Be Giants>[SINGING] No one in the world ever gets what they want, and that is beautiful. Everybody dies frustrated and sad, and that is beautiful. They want what they're--
<v They Might Be Giants>[唱歌] 世上没人能如愿以偿，那很美。每个人都在沮丧和悲伤中死去，那很美。他们想要他们所——

00:46:47.590 --> 00:46:54.550
<v Sarah Vowell>John Flansberg says he's often surprised by how much They Might Be Giants fans keep track of a song's evolution.
<v Sarah Vowell>约翰·弗兰斯伯格说他经常惊讶于“他们可能是巨人”的粉丝对歌曲的演变追踪得多么紧。

00:46:54.550 --> 00:47:25.720
<v John Flansberg>There's a song that's actually a bonus track on Factory Showroom. It's before track one, this song called "Token Back to Brooklyn." And I was talking to someone about this yesterday. And it was like this real-- It's the kind of thing that makes me feel like we are freak magnets because the guy was going, "In the song that was on Dial-a-Song, you said you wanted to kill your parents. And then you changed the lyric on the record--"
<v John Flansberg>有一首歌实际上是《工厂陈列室》(Factory Showroom)的奖励曲目。在第一轨之前，这首歌叫“回布鲁克林的代币”(Token Back to Brooklyn)。我昨天刚和某人谈起这个。这就像是一个真正的——这是那种让我觉得自己像个怪胎磁铁的事情，因为那个家伙说，“在“拨号歌曲”里的那首歌，你说你想杀掉你的父母。然后你在唱片上改了歌词——”

00:47:25.720 --> 00:47:26.640
<v John Linnell>To "tell our parents."
<v John Linnell>改为“告诉我们的父母”。

00:47:26.640 --> 00:47:34.990
<v John Flansberg>"To 'tell our parents.'" And I was just like, "Well--" And he was like, "And like, the reason you did that was, like, why?"
<v John Flansberg>“改为‘告诉我们的父母’。”我当时就像，“嗯——”然后他说，“而且，你那样做的理由是，为什么？”

00:47:34.990 --> 00:47:37.130
<v John Linnell>Because my mom was going to hear the record, maybe.
<v John Linnell>因为我妈可能会听到那张唱片，也许。

00:47:37.130 --> 00:47:47.760
<v John Flansberg>Yeah, and I explained to him that I really just didn't want to have any more songs about killing my parents.
<v John Flansberg>是啊，我向他解释说，我真的只是不想再有关于杀掉我父母的歌了。

00:47:47.760 --> 00:47:48.400
<v Sarah Vowell>Any more songs?
<v Sarah Vowell>再有歌？

00:47:48.400 --> 00:48:04.350
<v John Flansberg>Any more songs. Yeah, we've had songs about killing our parents before. And it really doesn't go over at holiday time. And so he was really disappointed. It was like finding out that Marilyn Manson--
<v John Flansberg>再有歌。是的，我们以前写过关于杀掉父母的歌。而在假日期间这真的不怎么讨喜。所以他真的很失望。这就像发现玛丽莲·曼森(Marilyn Manson)——

00:48:04.350 --> 00:48:05.650
<v Sarah Vowell>Was a nice guy?
<v Sarah Vowell>是个好人？

00:48:05.650 --> 00:48:16.920
<v John Flansberg>--takes the makeup off.
<v John Flansberg>——卸了妆。

00:48:16.920 --> 00:48:32.640
<v Sarah Vowell>One of my favorite They Might Be Giants songs isn't a song at all, but a mysterious recording on the album Miscellaneous T, of two unidentified callers discussing the merits of Dial-a-Song, whose number they found in the band's ad in the Village Voice .
<v Sarah Vowell>我最喜欢的“他们可能是巨人”的歌曲之一根本不是歌，而是专辑《Miscellaneous T》上的一段神秘录音，两个身份不明的来电者讨论“拨号歌曲”的优劣，他们在乐队于《村声》(Village Voice)上的广告里发现了这个号码。

00:48:32.640 --> 00:48:34.430
<v Female Caller>What do you make out of that recording?
<v Female Caller>你觉得那段录音怎么样？

00:48:34.430 --> 00:48:36.500
<v Male Caller>I don't know, [? Laurie, ?] I just don't--
<v Male Caller>我不知道，[劳里？]，我只是不——

00:48:36.500 --> 00:48:40.550
<v Female Caller>Some kind of singing. They sound like all kinds of people, right?
<v Female Caller>某种歌声。他们听起来像各种各样的人，对吧？

00:48:40.550 --> 00:48:40.970
<v Male Caller>Yeah.
<v Male Caller>是的。

00:48:40.970 --> 00:48:46.330
<v Female Caller>And then it says, "Another child is born in India every time you call this number," right?
<v Female Caller>然后它说，“每次你拨打这个号码，印度就有另一个孩子出生”，对吧？

00:48:46.330 --> 00:48:46.900
<v Male Caller>Yeah, right.
<v Male Caller>是啊，对。

00:48:46.900 --> 00:48:48.590
<v Female Caller>Does that make any sense to you?
<v Female Caller>这对你有什么意义吗？

00:48:48.590 --> 00:48:50.960
<v Male Caller>No, it doesn't make no sense--
<v Male Caller>不，那一点意义也没有——

00:48:50.960 --> 00:48:53.820
<v Female Caller>And the guy that spoke, I don't know who he is.
<v Female Caller>还有那个说话的家伙，我不知道他是谁。

00:48:53.820 --> 00:48:55.340
<v Male Caller>Yeah.
<v Male Caller>是啊。

00:48:55.340 --> 00:48:59.640
<v Female Caller>But it doesn't sound like no answering machine, right?
<v Female Caller>但这听起来不像什么答录机，对吧？

00:48:59.640 --> 00:49:04.180
<v Male Caller>No, it ain't an answering machine, because they're not saying anything. They're just--
<v Male Caller>不，那不是答录机，因为他们什么也没说。他们只是——

00:49:04.180 --> 00:49:14.910
<v Female Caller>But what does he get-- how does he make money on this, whatever he's advertising in the paper? This is the part that don't make no sense. That's where the--
<v Female Caller>但那个家伙得到什么——他怎么通过这个赚钱，无论他在报纸上广告的是什么？这是最没意义的部分。这就是那个——

00:49:14.910 --> 00:49:26.190
<v John Linnell>No, that was a woman who phoned up. And the reason why we had this long recording of her was that she was on a conference line with somebody else. This is another early--
<v John Linnell>不，那是个打电话进来的女人。我们有她这么长的录音是因为她和别人在电话会议线路上。这是另一个早期的——

00:49:26.190 --> 00:49:27.640
<v Sarah Vowell>Oh, and they were listening to it together?
<v Sarah Vowell>哦，他们是在一起听吗？

00:49:27.640 --> 00:49:45.340
<v John Linnell>--pop technology. Yeah, she and this guy she had phoned to tell him about Dial-a-Song were listening together. And then, when the machine finished its song, they had this conversation. And they had no way of disconnecting the third line. So we had about 45 minutes of them blabbering on.
<v John Linnell>——流行科技。是啊，她和那个她打电话去告诉他关于“拨号歌曲”的家伙一起听。然后，当机器放完歌，他们就聊了起来。他们没法断开第三条线。所以我们有了他们长达45分钟的胡言乱语。

00:49:45.340 --> 00:49:58.060
<v Female Caller>They've got the craziest things in that paper. But this one here, There Must Be Giants, it's called. And it says, call machine. And they've got the phone number.
<v Female Caller>他们在那张报纸上有最疯狂的东西。但这个在这儿，叫“也许存在巨人”。它说，呼叫机器。他们有电话号码。

00:49:58.060 --> 00:49:58.550
<v Male Caller>Yeah.
<v Male Caller>是啊。

00:49:58.550 --> 00:50:02.460
<v Female Caller>But what kind of money does he make? It don't make no sense.
<v Female Caller>但他赚什么钱？这根本没意义。

00:50:02.460 --> 00:50:03.370
<v Male Caller>[INAUDIBLE]
<v Male Caller>[听不清]

00:50:03.370 --> 00:50:05.120
<v Female Caller>Well, he don't make any money, right?
<v Female Caller>好吧，他根本赚不到钱，对吧？

00:50:05.120 --> 00:50:06.490
<v Male Caller>No.
<v Male Caller>不。

00:50:06.490 --> 00:50:08.120
<v Female Caller>Then he's a nut, right?
<v Female Caller>那他就是个疯子，对吧？

00:50:08.120 --> 00:50:11.430
<v Male Caller>Yeah, [INAUDIBLE].
<v Male Caller>是啊，[听不清]。

00:50:11.430 --> 00:50:29.480
<v Female Caller>Do you see any sense to that, There May Be Giants? That recording I have on [INAUDIBLE], did you hear it?
<v Female Caller>你觉得那有意义吗，“也许存在巨人”？我在[听不清]上的那段录音，你听了吗？

00:50:29.480 --> 00:50:35.920
<v Sarah Vowell>Well, I used to call it-- I would call it-- what's the motto? "Free, when you call from work."
<v Sarah Vowell>嗯，我过去常叫它——我会叫它——座右铭是什么？“从公司打来是免费的。”

00:50:35.920 --> 00:50:37.930
<v Sarah Vowell>(SUBJECT) JOHN FLANSBERG: "Free, when you call from work." Yeah, a lot of people do call it from work.
<v Sarah Vowell>(主题) 约翰·弗兰斯伯格：“从公司打来是免费的。”是啊，很多人确实是从公司打来的。

00:50:37.930 --> 00:50:54.080
<v Sarah Vowell>But I used to call it when I was really depressed, like so black and blue. And normally, in those situations, I'd try and feed it, and just get drunk and listen to Neil Young and cry myself to sleep. But on the healthier occasions--
<v Sarah Vowell>但我过去常在我真的很沮丧的时候打，就像遍体鳞伤的时候。通常，在这种情况下，我会试着喂养它，只是喝醉了听尼尔·杨(Neil Young)哭着入睡。但在比较健康的时候——

00:50:54.080 --> 00:50:57.050
<v John Flansberg>Wow. You should hang out with my downstairs neighbors.
<v John Flansberg>哇。你应该和我的楼下邻居聚聚。

00:50:57.050 --> 00:51:12.260
<v Sarah Vowell>On the healthier occasions, I'd go like, "I'm going to kick this thing and call Dial-a-Song." And I remember once being just so depressed and in tears. And I called it, and there was a song about how there was an ant crawling up my back. And it really-- it worked. It really cheered me up.
<v Sarah Vowell>在比较健康的时候，我会想，“我要踢开这一切，拨打“拨号歌曲”。”我记得有一次真是太沮丧了，泪流满面。我拨通了它，里面有一首歌关于一只蚂蚁爬上我的背。那真的——它奏效了。它真的让我振作了起来。

00:51:12.260 --> 00:51:30.820
<v John Linnell>It made you feel better. Yeah. See, some people wouldn't have that response to some of those songs. But I always felt like there was something at least happy about the music. For something so impersonal as a machine that you'd call up, that at least it was sort of merry in the music.
<v John Linnell>它让你感觉好多了。是啊。你看，有些人对那些歌可能不会有那种反应。但我总是觉得音乐里至少有些快乐的东西。对于像你打去的机器那样冷漠的东西，至少里面的音乐是某种程度上欢乐的。

00:51:30.820 --> 00:51:56.770
<v Sarah Vowell>It's better than that because it kind of includes both. Because it's very darkly merry. So it's kind of that sort of happiness that recognizes the dark side. So there's a whole other level. You had some new song at your show a couple of weeks ago that was very bright and happy, and it was about death, the death man or something like that. That's how I remember it.
<v Sarah Vowell>它比那更好，因为它某种程度上包含了两者。因为它非常阴暗地欢乐。所以它是一种承认黑暗面的快乐。所以还有另一个层面。你在几周前的演出里有一首新歌，非常明亮快乐，是关于死亡，死亡男什么的。我记得是这样。

00:51:56.770 --> 00:52:00.060
<v John Flansberg>Yeah. Yeah, the death man.
<v John Flansberg>是啊。是啊，死亡男。

00:52:00.060 --> 00:52:00.770
<v Sarah Vowell>I was picturing sickles.
<v Sarah Vowell>我当时在想象镰刀。

00:52:00.770 --> 00:52:38.450
<v John Linnell>That applies to most of our songs.
<v John Linnell>这适用于我们大多数的歌。

00:52:38.450 --> 00:53:06.390
<v Sarah Vowell>There's another reason besides this black humor that I call Dial-a-Song when I'm sad. I used to agree with John Linnell. I used to think it was just the deliciously dark They Might Be Giants music that cheered me up during those darkest hour Dial-a-Song calls. And that's part of it. But the thing that's just as reassuring about Dial-a-Song is the idea behind it, namely, that art, like life, doesn't always have to be a big production.
<v Sarah Vowell>除了这种黑色幽默，我会在伤心时拨打“拨号歌曲”还有另一个原因。我过去同意约翰·林内尔的观点。我过去认为只是那令人愉快地黑暗的“他们可能是巨人”音乐在那些最黑暗的“拨号歌曲”通话中让我振作了起来。那是一部分原因。但“拨号歌曲”同样令人安心的事情是它背后的想法，即，艺术，就像生活一样，并不总是需要大制作。

00:53:06.390 --> 00:53:17.770
<v Ira Glass>Sarah Vowell is the author of the book Take the Cannoli . The often busy Dial-a-Song number-- get out your pens-- 718-387-6962.
<v Ira Glass>莎拉·沃威尔是《带上卡诺里》(Take the Cannoli)一书的作者。那个经常忙线的“拨号歌曲”号码——拿出你们的笔——718-387-6962。

00:53:17.770 --> 00:54:08.500
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "I'M SICK" BY THEY MIGHT BE GIANTS]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - “我生病了”，他们可能是巨人]

00:54:08.500 --> 00:54:42.360
<v Ira Glass>Act Three, Phone as History. Well, we think of our phone calls and phone messages as so transient, as throwaway moments. Here is another example of a personal human history captured by phone machine, in this case, by Barrett Golding in Bozeman, Montana. The messages were about his father.
<v Ira Glass>第三幕，电话作为历史。嗯，我们认为我们的电话和留言是那么短暂，是转瞬即逝的时刻。这里是另一个通过电话录音机捕捉到的个人人类历史的例子，在这个案例中，是由蒙大拿州博兹曼的巴雷特·戈尔丁(Barrett Golding)捕捉到的。这些信息是关于他父亲的。

00:54:42.360 --> 00:54:47.990
<v Ira Glass>[BEEP] [BEEP] [BEEP] [BEEP]
<v Ira Glass>[哔] [哔] [哔] [哔]

00:54:47.990 --> 00:55:19.010
<v Bea Levine>Hello, Ralph. This is Bea Levine, Janette and Ben Brooks, and Lester and Charlotte Bloom. We're all here having a little afternoon tea. And we wanted to talk to you, but I guess you're out. We want to know how Ralph is. Would you try to call us back one day and let us know what's going on? We all want to know how Ralph is doing. We hope he's doing well. Let us know. Bye.
<v Bea Levine>你好，拉尔夫(Ralph)。我是碧·莱文(Bea Levine)、珍妮特(Janette)和本·布鲁克斯(Ben Brooks)，还有莱斯特(Lester)和夏洛特·布鲁姆(Charlotte Bloom)。我们大家在这儿喝下午茶。我们想和你谈谈，但我猜你出门了。我们想知道拉尔夫怎么样。你能试着回电告诉我们发生了什么吗？我们都想知道拉尔夫怎么样了。我们希望他一切都好。告诉我们。再见。

00:55:19.010 --> 00:55:20.930
<v Bea Levine>[BEEP]
<v Bea Levine>[哔]

00:55:20.930 --> 00:55:34.440
<v Bea Levine>(SUBJECT) WOMAN 1 Mr. Golding, this is [? Dr.  Spark's ?] office, confirming your appointment for Tuesday, July 12, at 4:00. You don't need to call us unless there's a problem. Thank you.
<v Bea Levine>(主题) 女人1 戈尔丁先生，这是[斯帕克医生(Dr. Spark)]的诊室，确认您7月12日星期二下午4点的预约。除非有问题，否则不需要打电话给我们。谢谢。

00:55:34.440 --> 00:55:36.780
<v Bea Levine>[BEEP]
<v Bea Levine>[哔]

00:55:36.780 --> 00:55:50.280
<v Rose Bernstein>Hi, this is Rose Bernstein. Just calling to welcome you back, and hope you had a very good time. And we'd like to say hi to you. Bye-bye.
<v Rose Bernstein>嗨，我是罗斯·伯恩斯坦(Rose Bernstein)。打电话只是想欢迎你回来，希望你过得愉快。我们想向你问好。拜拜。

00:55:50.280 --> 00:55:55.130
<v Rose Bernstein>[BEEP]
<v Rose Bernstein>[哔]

00:55:55.130 --> 00:56:09.720
<v Leah>It's Leah. I'm just heartsick over what happened. I'll get in touch with you later. [UNINTELLIGIBLE].
<v Leah>是利亚(Leah)。我为发生的事情感到心碎。我以后会再和你联系。[听不清]。

00:56:09.720 --> 00:56:12.590
<v Leah>[BEEP]
<v Leah>[哔]

00:56:12.590 --> 00:56:29.380
<v Liz>Hi, Mrs. Golding. It's Liz calling from Dr. Casey's office. I wanted just to give my condolences and tell you how sorry I am. I think we should touch base at some point. And I'll try calling you again later today. Bye.
<v Liz>嗨，戈尔丁夫人。我是利兹(Liz)，从凯西医生(Dr. Casey)的诊室打来的。我只是想表示慰问，并告诉你我有多么难过。我想我们在某个时候应该联系一下。我今天晚些时候会再试着给你打电话。再见。

00:56:29.380 --> 00:56:31.640
<v Liz>[BEEP]
<v Liz>[哔]

00:56:31.640 --> 00:56:43.910
<v Woman 2>Hello. It's 12:30. I just got a call in reference to Ralph. Please give me a call whenever you can. Thank you.
<v Woman 2>你好。现在是12点30分。我刚才接到了关于拉尔夫的电话。请在方便时给我回电。谢谢。

00:56:43.910 --> 00:56:55.130
<v Woman 2>[BEEP] [BEEP]
<v Woman 2>[哔] [哔]

00:56:55.130 --> 00:56:58.360
<v Woman 3>--going and is there anything I can do to help? We could--
<v Woman 3>——进行着，有什么我能帮忙的吗？我们可以——

00:56:58.360 --> 00:57:02.100
<v Woman 3>[INTERPOSING VOICES]
<v Woman 3>[干扰声]

00:57:02.100 --> 00:57:10.600
<v Woman 3>We're all very confused. So give me a call.
<v Woman 3>我们都很困惑。所以给我回个电话。

00:57:10.600 --> 00:57:17.550
<v Liz>I wanted just to give you my condolences and tell you how sorry I am.
<v Liz>我只是想表示慰问，并告诉你我有多么难过。

00:57:17.550 --> 00:57:33.060
<v Leah>It's Leah. I'm just heartsick over what happened. I'll get in touch with you later.
<v Leah>是利亚。我为发生的事情感到心碎。我以后会再和你联系。

00:57:33.060 --> 00:57:35.520
<v Leah>[TAPE STOPPING]
<v Leah>[磁带停止]

